plan CommandLine
title   Discover, run and inspect apps through flow
status  proposed
goal
    App authors discover and run Stories, inspect declarations and explain retained evidence
    through the installed flow binary without changing the application being inspected.
outcomes
    O1  Commands and selection
        flow exposes story list, story describe <story-id>, story run <story-id>, trace summarize
        <artifact|->, trace diff <left|-> <right|->, graph [<app-id/machine-id>], diff --base
        <git-revision>, check and explain. All support text and JSON output; graph also supports
        Mermaid. Text is the default. Help and version are conventional text.

        Source commands accept --project-root and --app. --app selects an exact app id. Without
        it, list lists all Stories and graph/check inspect all apps; commands selecting a Story
        or machine require a unique exact match. Ambiguous machine-id prefixes require --app;
        an explicit app must agree with the operand. Revision diff requires --app with several
        apps and compares current declarations against the selected revision, including additions
        and removals. Unknown revisions, apps or operands fail rather than inspecting another app.

        story list returns declared identities; describe returns actors, commands and checkpoints.
        graph uses @plan.Inspection.O6. explain accepts a machine, state path and public event,
        or a domain with an optional --read. Static explanations show possible transitions,
        denials and fallthrough without evaluating guards, or domain reads and their impact.
        explain --trace <artifact|-> --at <checkpoint> uses recorded evidence only.

        Invalid, missing, repeated or conflicting arguments fail before application loading.
        Selectors are data and never execute expressions. Artifact-only commands accept no source
        selection options and load no application or config. At most one input may be stdin (-).
    O2  Execution and safe trace output
        Only story run starts application work. Discovery, graph, check, static explain and
        revision diff may load declarations, including lazy entries, but activate no actors,
        services, fixtures or machine callbacks. Running a Story through flow produces the same
        checkpoints, outcomes and failure evidence as running it directly under @plan.Testing.O1.
        Cancellation and cleanup evidence follow @plan.Testing.O8.

        Artifact inputs are validated under @plan.Inspection.O4, including bounds while reading
        stdin. Invalid or incompatible artifacts fail before a query reports an answer.
        story run --trace-output <path> checks destination usability before starting the Story.
        Existing entries reject unless --overwrite is supplied; --overwrite without a destination
        rejects. Replacement of a symlink changes the link entry, never its target.
        Publication is atomic: readers see a complete artifact; pre-publication failure leaves
        an existing destination unchanged. A racing creator is never overwritten without consent.
        A published artifact is never rolled back. Temporary files are cleaned on every exit,
        and cleanup failures are reported. Unsupported safe publication fails without truncation.

        Requested trace output is attempted even after execution failure; the execution failure
        remains primary and drain, encoding, write and cleanup failures remain visible.
        Interruption stops later Story commands, waits for bounded disposal under
        @plan.Runtime.O6, and attempts requested partial trace output before reporting interruption.
        Partial traces retain accepted evidence and cleanup truth and never fabricate an end.
        Cleanup or trace-output failure cannot replace interruption as the primary result.
        Forced process termination provides no cleanup guarantee.
    O3  Results for people and automation
        Text and JSON describe the same result. JSON supplies version: 1, the command leaf,
        status, data and ordered diagnostics; absent data is null. Status is completed, equal,
        different, incomplete or failed. Diagnostic values follow @plan.Diagnostics.O2;
        execution failures use FlowError under @plan.Diagnostics.O1.

        Successful invocations and comparison/check findings emit one newline-terminated document
        on stdout. Invocation failures emit one document on stderr. Results never mix with loader
        console output or include ANSI in JSON/non-interactive text. Exported sensitive values
        follow @plan.Inspection.O5; raw causes, stacks and private adapter authority never appear.
        A broken output pipe fails without recursively attempting to print another error.

        Completed commands, warning-only checks, equal comparisons and explanations/summaries
        with insufficient evidence exit 0. Differences, incomplete trace comparisons and checks
        with errors exit 1 and remain stdout results. Usage, loading, artifact, Story execution,
        cleanup and I/O failures exit 2. Interruption reports failure only after cleanup.
        Product failures recorded as Story issues do not alone fail the invocation; a completed
        Story has an end and no execution failure. Cleanup failure cannot report success.
    O4  Trace queries
        trace summarize reports counts and an ordered timeline over the retained evidence.
        --actor selects an exact actor; --view issues selects issues rather than the timeline.
        --read <domain.entry> reports repairs and their causes; --entity <Entity:key> reports
        field sources, candidates, predictions, uncertainty and queued work. Entity keys split
        at the first colon, preserving later colons. --sequence selects a nonnegative safe-integer
        cut for entity queries only. No selector means the whole retained timeline.

        Evidence explain uses the selected checkpoint to report recorded eligibility or domain
        repair evidence without executing current code or inventing reasons for unconsidered
        candidates. An absent selector/checkpoint in a truncated window is insufficient evidence,
        not proof of absence. Unknown selectors in complete evidence fail. Answers include the
        selection and completeness marker. Projection and comparison semantics follow
        @plan.Inspection.O6.

        trace diff optionally selects sections with --section; omission compares all sections.
        Equal requires complete matching evidence. Differing retained facts prove difference;
        matching truncated evidence is incomplete and never proves equality. Both completeness
        markers accompany the result. Incompatible identities fail rather than report difference.
        Independently redacted values never establish equality or difference without evidence.
    O5  Static checks
        flow check checks the complete declared graph, including lazy entries, without running
        application callbacks. It reports invalid declarations, wiring, ownership and recorded
        domain rules as errors, including violations that untyped input would otherwise bypass.
        Domain validity follows @plan.EntityDomains.O1. Deny codes come from declared types;
        missing complete type facts fails instead of claiming a complete analysis.

        Warnings identify likely stuck/unreachable states, unhandled events, unensured send
        targets, send cycles, unexercised denials, ambiguous composite ids, duplicated services,
        untracked entity/list data, sensitive SSR fields and eager or opaque lazy loading.
        Each finding includes severity, a stable rule reason, its actual path and actionable help.
        Warnings alone do not fail the check. Failure to load a graph is an invocation failure;
        findings cannot certify arbitrary runtime callbacks or runtime correctness.
    O6  Configuration discovery
        At --project-root (default cwd), flow loads only flow.config.ts, whose default export is
        defineConfig({ apps: [{ app, stories }] }). stories is a root-relative glob discovering
        exported branded Stories with their own ids. The root must contain package.json;
        discovery never searches ancestors or silently chooses another config.

        Missing or unloadable config fails. Empty config, missing apps/Stories, duplicate app ids
        or Story ids within an app, foreign setups/machines and structural lookalikes reject
        before acquiring execution resources. Flow and Effect values must come from the exact
        package instances used by the CLI. Focused Stories must belong to their configured app.

        Selected config, Story and project entry files must resolve inside the project root after
        symlinks; escaping entries reject before execution. Revision diff applies the same rule
        inside its selected revision. Flow writes no caches or generated bundles into the project
        and removes its external temporary files on success, failure and interruption.
        Loading local TypeScript trusts that code with host authority; it is not a sandbox.
verification
    V1  Installed CLI tests prove command selection, invalid-input rejection and inert discovery.
    V2  Tests prove direct/CLI Story parity, interruption cleanup and truthful partial traces.
    V3  Tests prove atomic output, no-clobber races, symlink safety and destination preservation.
    V4  Tests prove JSON/text agreement, exclusive result streams, redaction and exit mapping.
    V5  Trace tests prove complete, different, truncated and incompatible query results.
    V6  Check fixtures prove full-graph errors, actionable warnings and absent type-fact failure.
    V7  Tests prove config identity, root containment, ancestor refusal and no project writes.
questions
    Q1  Deferred to build time
        Exact flag grammar and trace section vocabulary.
        Signal-specific exits and repeated-signal handling.
        Atomic publication primitives, temporary-file permissions and flush protocol.
        Loader, bundler and revision-isolation strategy.
        Console capture bounds and output buffering strategy.
        Exact warning slugs and static-analysis implementation.
